module

public import FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«344»

/-!
# Erdős Problem 344, the `√(2N)` variant: such sets are subcomplete

Generated by an AI agent and machine-checked; not reviewed by a human expert.

## Statement

`main` has exactly the type of the upstream theorem `Erdos344.erdos_344.variants.sqrt_two` (Formal
Conjectures, `FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/344.lean`, commit 9097e2f9, where it is marked
`category research open`):

    ∀ A : Set ℕ, (∀ᶠ N : ℕ in atTop, Real.sqrt (2 * N) ≤ A.countUpTo N) → IsSubcomplete A

In words: if `A ⊆ ℕ` has at least `√(2N)` elements in `{1, ..., N}` for all large `N`, then the set
of sums of finitely many distinct elements of `A` contains an infinite arithmetic progression. The
axioms used are `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.

An earlier, longer version of this file (not included in this repository) also had a
theorem `headline` with the type of `Erdos344.erdos_344`. It follows from `main` in a few lines
(with `C = √2`), but it is known mathematics with a public Lean proof (see below), so it is dropped
here.

## Proof outline

The human-readable proof is in `Erdos344.md`, with the same lemma numbers. List `A` as
`a 0 < a 1 < ...`. The hypothesis gives `2 * a k ≤ k ^ 2 + 2` for large `k` (Lemma 1). `P(J)` is the
set of sums of `a i` over finite sets of indices in `J`; `J` is good if every `a m`, `m ∈ J`, is at
most a constant plus the sum of the earlier `a i`, `i ∈ J`. If `C` is infinite and good, then `P(C)`
has bounded gaps (Lemma 2), and so have the multiples of `d` in `P(C)` (Lemma 3). So it is enough to
find disjoint `J`, `C` with `C` infinite and good, such that `P(J)` contains arbitrarily long
progressions with one fixed difference (Lemma 4).

* Case 1: some difference `a (i + 1) - a i = d` occurs infinitely often. Let `B` be a very sparse
  set of such index pairs `i, i + 1`. Choosing one index from each pair gives the progressions. The
  complement of `B` is good: if not almost all differences are `1`, then `a i ≥ i + 4` for large
  `i`, and the sum of `i + 4` over the `i < m` outside `B` is at least `m ^ 2 / 2 - O(1)`, which is
  at least `a m - O(1)`. Only here is the constant `√2` needed: Case 2 would work with any bound
  `a m = O(m²)` and a larger number in place of `11` (on paper only; `case2` as compiled assumes
  `2 * a k ≤ k ^ 2 + 2`).
* Case 2: `a (i + 1) ≥ a i + 11` for all large `i`. Then the four index classes modulo 4 are good.
  Two of them give syndetic sets `X`, `Y` of sums, the third a sequence `b_i = O(i²)`, and the
  fourth is `C`. By Proposition 15, the sumset of `X`, `Y` and the set of sums of distinct `b_i`
  contains the progressions. Its proof fixes `n, d, L, m, R, D` in this order. Start from the set
  `Z_0` of all elements of `X` below `(G + 1) R`, of which there are at least `R`, and add the `b_i`
  one by one, `Z_{i+1} = Z_i ∪ (Z_i + b_i)`. For fixed `i` and large `R`, `|Z_i| / R` is at most
  about `G + 1`. So there is a fixed `n` such that for every `m ≥ n` some `R` makes `|Z_m| - |Z_n|`
  a small fraction of `R`. Put `d = (κ n)!`, where `κ` depends only on the `b_i`. Given the length
  `L`, take a suitable large `m`, then `R` as above. By the pigeonhole principle at geometric
  scales, applied to the `b_i` which add few elements, there is `D < κ n` such that for all `x ≤ m`
  most `z ∈ Z_n` have `z + x D ∈ Z_m`. As `D` divides `d`, the shifts `j d`, `j ≤ L`, are of this
  form. One popular sum `t` of `Y + Z_n` then gives all `t + j d`.

## Scope and priority

* This is an affirmative answer to a variant of Problem 344, the one with the constant `√2`. The
  headline problem (some absolute constant) was solved long ago; see below. This file must not be
  announced as a solution of Erdős Problem 344.
* The `√(2N)` statement is new as far as known: two independent searches on 24-25 Nov 2026 found no
  earlier proof in print, online or in Lean. Both had gaps (not read: the page and thread on the
  Erdős problems site, Section 6 of the Erdős-Graham monograph of 1980, Szemerédi's 2016 survey, Fox
  and Knapp 2013; Google Scholar was not used; some database queries failed; the second search did
  not re-open Erdős 1961/62 and Chen 2003). So: likely new, not certainly new. It was recorded as
  open on those dates by Formal Conjectures
  (https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/pull/6877),
  https://erdosproblems.ai/wiki/problems/additive_bases/E0344 (source in
  https://github.com/plasma-ai/erdos) and https://github.com/facebookresearch/atlas-lean.
* The formal statement asks for the bound for all large `N`; the informal "for all `N`" is
  impossible at `N = 1`. This is the right repair and the stronger theorem. No degenerate case is
  used. The set `{1, 2, 3, 5, 9, 13, ...}` with `a k = ⌊(k ^ 2 + 2) / 2⌋` for `k ≥ 2` satisfies the
  hypothesis (a reviewer of the earlier file checked this in Lean).
* No constant below `√2` would do (a remark of Erdős, 1961/62, p. 346; Remark 2 of `Erdos344.md`;
  not formalized). `√(2N)` is not claimed to be the exact threshold.
* The question is Folkman's (1966, p. 655): if `a_n ≤ M n ^ 2` for large `n`, where `M ≤ 1/2`, is
  `A` subcomplete? `main` gives the answer yes for `M < 1/2` (on paper, not formalized here), not
  literally for `M = 1/2`. The proof also works for `2 * a k ≤ k ^ 2 + c * k + 2` with any constant
  `c`, which covers `M = 1/2`; see Remark 1 of `Erdos344.md`. That is not part of this file. A copy
  of this file with these changes compiles, but it is not included in this repository and has
  not been reviewed.
* How much follows from published work (paper deductions by a reviewer, not formalized). V.
  Bergelson and D. Simmons, "New examples of complete sets, with connections to a Diophantine
  theorem of Furstenberg", Acta Arith. 177 (2017) 101-131, https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.02208, Main
  Theorem 2.1: if `B₁, B₂, B₃, C ⊆ ℕ` are disjoint, (I) each `n ∈ B_i` is at most a constant plus
  the sum of the smaller elements of `B_i`, (II) `∑_{n ∈ C} ‖n α‖ = ∞` for every irrational `α`, and
  (III) the sums of distinct elements of `C` meet every residue class modulo every `q ≥ 1`, then all
  large integers are sums of distinct elements of the four sets. If the gcd of `{a i : i ≥ m}` is
  the same for all large `m`, this and a lemma on sequences of size `O(i²)` give Case 2 (for (III)
  see their Remarks 2.8-2.10; according to Fan, p. 8, it is also in Graham 1964, Lemma 3). The
  reviewer did this for the earlier version of Case 2 (`a k ≥ 100 * k`, five index classes). It
  looks the same for the four classes used here, but it was not redone in detail. If the gcd tends
  to infinity with `m` (blocks of multiples of `j!`, for instance), (III) can fail and the theorem
  does not apply as it stands. Case 1 is close to Lemma 6.1 of S. Fan, "Strongly complete sets and a
  conjecture of Erdős", https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14071; the added step is that a sparse family of
  pairs can be removed. (Fan's Corollary 6.3 finds such pairs under conditions which the sets here
  need not satisfy: a fixed number of elements in every interval `(ρ ^ k, ρ ^ (k + 1)]` and a gcd
  condition.) No published theorem that implies `main` was found, but an expert could reasonably
  call it a modest assembly of known tools.
* Remarks in print that conflict or may conflict. N. Hegyvári, "On the representation of integers
  as sums of distinct terms from a fixed set", Acta Arith. 92 (2000) 99-104,
  https://doi.org/10.4064/aa-92-2-99-104, p. 99 (repeated in his dissertation of 2017, p. 66,
  https://real-d.mtak.hu/1038/7/dc_1260_16_doktori_mu.pdf, and, without a citation at that sentence,
  in https://oeis.org/wiki/Complete_sequences), says that a set with `A(n) > √(2n)` which is not
  subcomplete is easy to construct; no construction is given. For all large `n` this contradicts
  `main`, and the usual obstruction (`a_{k+1} - (a_1 + ... + a_k)` unbounded) cannot occur there, so
  it looks like a slip for `c√n` with `c < √2`. The author was not asked. Y.-G. Chen (2003,
  section 4) states a belief, without proof, that for every `K > 0` there are `n_0` and a set `A`,
  not subcomplete, with `|A ∩ [1, n]| ≥ K√n` for all `n ≥ n_0`. This conflicts with `main` and
  already with Szemerédi and Vu.
* To be checked by an expert: Hegyvári's remark; how much is a corollary of Bergelson-Simmons and
  Fan; Folkman as the source of the question, and his case `M = 1/2`; the unread places above.

## Earlier work on the headline statement

The exponent or order in brackets is what suffices there. P. Erdős, Acta Arith. 7 (1961/62) 345-354,
https://users.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1961-18.pdf (exponent `(√5 - 1)/2`); J. Folkman, Canad. J. Math. 18
(1966) 643-655, https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1966-065-2 (`N ^ (1/2 + ε)`); N. Hegyvári (2000, above)
and T. Łuczak and T. Schoen, Acta Arith. 95 (2000) 225-229, https://doi.org/10.4064/aa-95-3-225-229
(order `√(N log N)`); Y.-G. Chen, "On subset sums of a fixed set", Acta Arith. 106 (2003) 207-211,
https://doi.org/10.4064/aa106-3-1, Theorem 1 (some `C > 1` such that `|A ∩ [1, n]| > C√n` for all
`n > C ^ 2` suffices; this threshold is the same for all `A`, so as stated it is weaker than the
eventual form used upstream; the constant is very large); E. Szemerédi and V. H. Vu, Ann. of Math.
163 (2006) 1-35, https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2006.163.1, Corollary 1.4, and J. Amer. Math. Soc.
19 (2006) 119-169, https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0507539, Theorem 9.4 (unspecified constant; they say
that Chen also proved their Theorem 9.4). A Lean proof with a large constant:
https://github.com/plby/lean-proofs, src/latest/ErdosProblems/Erdos344.lean (formal authors "Codex"
and "GPT-5.6 Sol").

